{"title": "CSCI-420: Principles of Data Mining", "learningObjectives": [
        "Define the knowledge discovery process and distinguish descriptive data mining from predictive modeling.",
        "Explain data exploration and cleaning techniques, including handling missingness, duplicates, and inconsistent representation.",
        "Apply data mining algorithms for grouping, dimensionality reduction, classification, regression, and clustering to describe datasets."
      ], "beats": [
        {
          "title": "Course Foundations and Scope",
          "claim": "Real-world datasets are often large and can be stored in either main memory or a database.",
          "example": "Student teams will load a 100,000-row customer transaction dataset into a relational database for initial exploration.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a split-screen graphic showing a database schema on the left and an in-memory DataFrame on the right, with arrows indicating data flow between storage types.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-420"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Knowledge Discovery Process",
          "claim": "The knowledge discovery process involves using data mining to describe datasets rather than making predictions.",
          "example": "Instructors will demonstrate how clustering customer purchase patterns reveals market segments without forecasting future sales.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a flowchart labeled 'Knowledge Discovery Process' with nodes for data collection, cleaning, analysis, and description, explicitly excluding a 'prediction' node.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-420"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Data Exploration and Cleaning",
          "claim": "Data quality analysis makes missingness, duplicates, inconsistent representation, integrity constraints, and sampling explicit.",
          "example": "Students will run a missingness report on a survey dataset, flagging 15% null values in the 'income' column and removing 3% duplicate records.",
          "visualDirection": "Render a heatmap of missing values across columns, with a callout box highlighting the 'income' column and a duplicate count badge.",
          "sourceIds": ["postgresql-documentation"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Data Preparation Workflows",
          "claim": "Data-analysis workflows separate data preparation, exploratory analysis, training, validation, testing, model selection, and evaluation.",
          "example": "A pipeline will be built where raw data undergoes imputation and normalization before entering the exploratory analysis phase.",
          "visualDirection": "Illustrate a horizontal pipeline diagram with seven distinct stages, each labeled with a workflow phase and connected by directional arrows.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Supervised and Unsupervised Methods",
          "claim": "Supervised methods learn mappings from examples paired with target labels, while unsupervised methods identify structure without targets.",
          "example": "Students will compare a decision tree trained on labeled customer churn data against a k-means clustering run on the same unlabeled features.",
          "visualDirection": "Display two side-by-side scatter plots: one with labeled points and a decision boundary, the other with unlabeled points and cluster centroids.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Preprocessing and Leakage Prevention",
          "claim": "Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted training data to avoid leakage.",
          "example": "Instructors will demonstrate scaling a feature using only the training set statistics, then applying the same transformation to the validation set.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a before-and-after comparison of a feature distribution, with a red 'leakage' warning icon over a graph where validation statistics were incorrectly used for scaling.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Pipelines and Consistency",
          "claim": "Pipelines combine transformations and estimators so the same operations are applied consistently.",
          "example": "A single pipeline object will chain standardization, imputation, and clustering, ensuring identical operations run on every data split.",
          "visualDirection": "Render a stacked block diagram where each layer represents a transformation or estimator, with a 'consistent application' label spanning the entire stack.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Cross-Validation and Sampling",
          "claim": "Cross-validation repeats training and validation across defined splits to assess model stability.",
          "example": "Students will execute a 5-fold cross-validation routine, rotating the validation set through each dataset partition and recording accuracy scores.",
          "visualDirection": "Illustrate a circular diagram with five segments, each labeled with a fold number, and arrows showing data rotation between training and validation phases.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Algorithm Selection and Evaluation",
          "claim": "Grouping, dimensionality reduction, classification, regression, and clustering answer different questions and require appropriate assumptions and metrics.",
          "example": "Instructors will match a regression model to predicting continuous revenue and a clustering algorithm to segment user behavior, explaining the distinct evaluation metrics for each.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a decision tree flowchart where each branch leads to an algorithm type, with corresponding metric badges (e.g., RMSE, silhouette score) attached to each leaf.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
          "claim": "Distribution shift, missing data, overfitting, and unexamined sampling choices can invalidate conclusions.",
          "example": "Students will complete a checklist verifying data provenance, checking for distribution drift between train and test sets, and documenting sampling methodology.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a clean checklist graphic with five checkboxes, each paired with a brief validation step, and a final 'valid conclusions' stamp icon.",
          "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
        }
      ]}
